<article class="zzz"><header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Procter &amp; Gamble / ZzzQuil</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">ZzzQuil</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">Sleep-first UX</span><span class="case-meta__role">Senior UX Strategist</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Product Strategy &amp; Mobile UX</p><h1>ZzzQuil<br /><span class="heading-accent">Sleep</span><br />App</h1></div><p>As Senior UX Strategist through Pocket Made, I led ChrowmDesigns' UX strategy for ZzzQuil Sleepmate and its companion shopping website, covering color, information architecture, and interaction design.</p></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "Research"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Visual system"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Experience"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Results"}]} /><figure class="hero"><img src="/images/zzzquil_hero.webp" alt="Person asleep in calming purple bedroom light" loading="eager" decoding="async" /></figure>
<section class="research" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p>01 / The context</p><h2>An app for bedtime.<br />A site for shopping.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>The app supported listening and bedtime settings; the website supported product discovery and purchasing, with handoffs for post-purchase downloads and replenishment.</p></div><div class="research__images"><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil01.webp" alt="ZzzQuil desktop site in bedroom context" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil02.webp" alt="ZzzQuil mobile sleep app" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil03.webp" alt="ZzzQuil responsive website system" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="zzz-detail" aria-labelledby="zzz-research-heading"><div><p class="zzz-detail__label">Research &amp; discovery</p><h2 id="zzz-research-heading">What belonged on<br />the screen?</h2></div><div><p>I reviewed contrast ratios, dark-mode requirements, and fourteen sleep and wellness apps to separate brand expression from functional controls.</p></div></section>
<section class="palette" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><div class="palette__head"><p>02 / A new visual language</p><h2>Making the purple<br /><span class="heading-accent">work on screen.</span></h2></div><div class="copy"><p>Adjusting purple saturation and luminance addressed hierarchy beyond a simple color inversion, with readability against dark surfaces as the goal.</p><p>Liquid-inspired gradients, photography, and illustration carried the brand while navigation and backgrounds stayed restrained. P&amp;G's brand team collaborated on imagery and photography direction, which I reviewed against the interface system.</p></div><div class="palette__images"><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil04.webp" alt="ZzzQuil product image and restful palette" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil05.webp" alt="Lavender visual language reference" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil06.webp" alt="ZzzQuil product visual language" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="platform" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p>03 / Content first</p><h2>Platform Architecture &amp;<br />Design System</h2></div><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil_phones-01.webp" alt="ZzzQuil sleep app screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="copy"><p>Navigation receded during listening, and separate soundscape volume controls let users adjust layered audio without leaving it.</p><p>A visual clock calculated sleep duration from bedtime and wake time, while notification preferences and detailed settings remained available without crowding setup.</p></div></section>
<section class="results" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><div><p>04 / Results</p><h2>Results &amp;<br />Usage Patterns</h2></div><div class="numbers"><div><b>#1</b><span>soundscape mixer became most-used feature</span></div><div><b>AA</b><span>WCAG 2.1 design target</span></div><div><b>3</b><span>platforms in one system</span></div></div><div class="copy"><p>The existing project record identifies the mixer as the most-used feature within thirty days of launch; it does not establish that navigation placement caused its popularity. WCAG 2.1 AA remained a design target, not a verified compliance result.</p><p>The shared system covered iOS, Android, and responsive web, documenting buttons, cards, type, states, and behavior for extension by the development team.</p></div></section><section class="zzz-detail" aria-labelledby="zzz-handoff-heading"><div><p class="zzz-detail__label">Handoff &amp; reflection</p><h2 id="zzz-handoff-heading">Keep the system useful<br />after delivery.</h2></div><div><p>Platform constraints required shared rules rather than identical screens.</p><p>I would test the app within participants' bedtime routines to assess the color and interaction decisions in the setting daytime reviews leave out.</p></div></section></article>
